  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934287/assets/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$",
